    {
      "id": "msg_1791730270631_a",
      "conversationId": "conv_1791730076433_u1nxm",
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To give you the best advice, I need to see your current resume. Since I don't have it yet, I can give you a comprehensive checklist of the \"next level\" upgrades you can make.\n\nHowever, if you paste your resume here, I can give you specific feedback.\n\nIn the meantime, here is the **Resume Upgrade Framework**—the specific things that move a resume from \"basic\" to \"next level\":\n\n### 1. Quantify Everything (The Biggest Upgrade)\nMost people list duties; \"next level\" resumes list achievements with numbers.\n*   **Before:** \"Responsible for sales and customer service.\"\n*   **After:** \"Increased sales revenue by 20% year-over-year and maintained a 98% customer satisfaction rating.\"\n*   **What to add:** Percentages, dollar amounts, time saved, number of people managed, etc.\n\n### 2. Replace \"Responsibilities\" with \"Accomplishments\"\nJob descriptions often just list what you were *supposed* to do. You need to show what you *actually* achieved.\n*   **Action Verbs:** Start every bullet point with a strong verb (e.g., *Spearheaded, Optimized, Launched, Spearheaded*).\n*   **The \"So What?\" Test:** After writing a bullet point, ask yourself: \"So what? Why does this matter?\"\n\n### 3. Optimize for ATS (Applicant Tracking Systems)\nMany resumes are rejected by robots before a human ever sees them.\n*   **Keywords:** Mirror the language from the job description. If they ask for \"Project Management\" and \"Budgeting,\" make sure those exact words appear in your skills section and experience.\n*   **Formatting:** Avoid columns, graphics, or weird fonts. Use standard headings (Experience, Education, Skills).\n\n### 4. The \"Summary\" Section (Instead of Objective)\nOld resumes start with \"Objective: Seeking a position...\" New resumes start with a **Professional Summary**.\n*   **What it is:** 3-4 lines at the top highlighting your years of experience, key skills, and what you bring to the table.\n*   **Example:** \"Results-oriented Marketing Manager with 10+ years of experience in digital strategy and brand growth. Proven track record of increasing online engagement by 150%.\"\n\n### 5. Skills Section: Be Specific\nDon't just list \"Microsoft Office.\" List the specific tools you use.\n*   **Before:** \"Excel, Word, PowerPoint.\"\n*   **After:** \"Advanced Excel (Pivot Tables, VLOOKUP), Adobe Creative Suite (Photoshop, InDesign), CRM Software (Salesforce).\"\n\n### 6. Clean Up the Design\nA \"next level\" resume looks modern and easy to read.\n*   **Font:** Use clean, modern fonts like Helvetica, Calibri, or Garamond.\n*   **White Space:** Don't cram everything in. Use margins and spacing to make it breathe.\n*   **Length:** 1 page for < 10 years experience; 2 pages is acceptable for senior-level roles.\n\n---\n\n**Ready to level up?**\n**Paste your current resume below**, and I will:\n1. Rewrite your bullet points with quantifiable achievements.\n2. Write a strong Professional Summary for you.\n3. Optimize it for keywords based on a job you want.\n\n*What is your current job/industry?* (This helps me tailor the advice better).",
